            <th>Key Provisions & Mandates</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Compensation for Delay in Credit Info Updation</strong></td>
            <td>RBI/2023-24/72 (DoR.FIN.REC.48/20.16.003/2023-24)</td>
            <td>26 April 2024</td>
            <td>30-day dispute resolution window (21 days for bank + 9 days for CIC); statutory compensation of ₹100 per calendar day of delay.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Credit & Debit Card Issuance and Conduct Directions</strong></td>
            <td>RBI/2022-23/92 (DoR.FSD.REC.27/24.01.041/2022-23)</td>
            <td>01 July 2022</td>
            <td>Mandatory MAD warning on bills; one-time right to modify billing cycle; 3-day grace period before late payment reporting.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Master Direction – Fair Practices Code for Lenders</strong></td>
            <td>Updated 2024 (Master Direction)</td>
            <td>Active</td>
            <td>Calling window strictly restricted to 8:00 AM – 7:00 PM; complete prohibition on verbal harassment, intimidation, and third-party intrusions.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Reserve Bank – Integrated Ombudsman Scheme</strong></td>
            <td>RB-IOS 2021</td>
            <td>12 Nov 2021</td>
            <td>Centralized CMS portal complaint mechanism; compensation up to ₹20 Lakhs for consequential loss and ₹1 Lakh for mental harassment.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Guidelines on Digital Lending</strong></td>
            <td>DOR.CRE.REC.66/21.07.001/2022-23</td>
            <td>02 Sept 2022</td>
            <td>Ban on smartphone contact list and photo gallery access; mandatory Key Fact Statement (KFS); cooling-off look-up period.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Pre-payment Charges on Loans Directions</strong></td>
            <td>Updated Directives</td>
            <td>Active</td>
            <td>Zero foreclosure and prepayment charges on all floating-rate term loans to individual borrowers for non-business purposes.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Compromise Settlements & Technical Write-offs</strong></td>
            <td>DOR.STR.REC.20/21.04.048/2023-24</td>
            <td>08 June 2023</td>
            <td>Board-approved compromise settlement framework; cooling periods; mandatory accurate reporting of haircut write-offs to CICs.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>2. Central Acts of Parliament</h2>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Statute</th>
            <th>Act Number</th>
            <th>Key Sections Covered</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>SARFAESI Act, 2002</strong></td>
            <td>Act No. 54 of 2002</td>
            <td>Section 13(2) (60-day notice), Section 13(3A) (representation), Section 13(4) (possession), Section 17 (DRT appeal), Section 34 (civil court bar).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881</strong></td>
            <td>Act No. 26 of 1881</td>
            <td>Section 138 (cheque dishonour), Section 141 (offences by companies), Section 142 (limitation period), Section 147 (compoundability).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Payment and Settlement Systems Act, 2007</strong></td>
            <td>Act No. 51 of 2007</td>
            <td>Section 25 (dishonour of electronic funds transfer / eNACH mandates carrying equal criminal penalties to Sec 138 NI Act).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Credit Information Companies (Regulation) Act, 2005</strong></td>
            <td>Act No. 30 of 2005</td>
            <td>Section 21 (alteration and correction of credit data), Section 15 (membership obligations), 7-year historical reporting standards.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Income Tax Act, 1961</strong></td>
            <td>Act No. 43 of 1961</td>
            <td>Section 269SS (acceptance of cash loan >= ₹20k), Section 269T (repayment of cash loan >= ₹20k), Section 271D/271E (100% penalty).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Indian Contract Act, 1872</strong></td>
            <td>Act No. 9 of 1872</td>
            <td>Section 126 (guarantee), Section 128 (co-extensive liability of surety), Section 140 (surety's right of subrogation).</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>3. Judicial Precedents (Supreme Court of India)</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Mardia Chemicals Ltd. v. Union of India (2004) 4 SCC 311:</strong> Established that secured creditors must provide a reasoned reply to borrower objections under SARFAESI Section 13(3A) before taking possession.</li>
      <li><strong>State Bank of India v. Jah Developers (P) Ltd. (2019) 6 SCC 787:</strong> Mandated strict natural justice principles and mandatory representation before borrower classification as a Wilful Defaulter.</li>
      <li><strong>Bank of Bihar Ltd. v. Damodar Prasad (1969) 1 SCR 620:</strong> Established that the liability of a loan guarantor is immediate and co-extensive with the principal debtor.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
